{"name":"Wild Violet","hex":"#63209b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wild","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Untamed; not domesticated.","Especially, being of the wild type: being of an unbroken ancestral line of undomesticated animals, as opposed to being feral, being an undomesticated animal whose ancestors were domesticated."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Inaccurately; not on target.","Intended to be synchronized with film or video but recorded separately."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wild"},{"term":"violet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant or flower of the genus Viola, especially the fragrant Viola odorata;  similar-looking plants and flowers.","A person thought to resemble V. odorata, especially in its beauty and delicacy."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a violet colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/violet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Viola (plant)","blocks":[{"text":"Viola, commonly known as the violets, is a genus of flowering plants in the family Violaceae. It is the largest genus in the family, containing over 680 species. Most species are found in the temperate Northern Hemisphere; however, some are also found in widely divergent areas such as Hawaii, Australasia, and the Andes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Viola_reichenbachiana_LC0128.jpg/330px-Viola_reichenbachiana_LC0128.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viola_(plant)"},{"title":"Violet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is the color of light at the short wavelength end of the visible spectrum. It is one of the seven colors that Isaac Newton labeled when dividing the spectrum of visible light in 1704. Violet light has a wavelength between approximately 380 and 435 nanometers. The color's name is derived from the Viola genus of flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violet_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Akkerviooltje_opengesprongen_doosvrucht.jpg/500px-Akkerviooltje_opengesprongen_doosvrucht.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Akkerviooltje_opengesprongen_doosvrucht.jpg/500px-Akkerviooltje_opengesprongen_doosvrucht.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Opened seed capsule of Viola arvensis (field pansy, Melanium), showing the seeds","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAkkerviooltje_opengesprongen_doosvrucht.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Linnaeus_Viola.png/500px-Linnaeus_Viola.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Linnaeus_Viola.png/1280px-Linnaeus_Viola.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Linnaeus_Viola.png/500px-Linnaeus_Viola.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Linnaeus' original description (1753)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALinnaeus_Viola.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Viola_canina_100.jpg/250px-Viola_canina_100.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/98/Viola_canina_100.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Viola_canina_100.jpg/250px-Viola_canina_100.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c